 ],
 "literal": "Buds have matured, the jasmine, along with jasmine / beauty has reached fullness in the cool rainy season's wide forest field / He who made my bright jewels slip has not returned / Evening has arrived, targeting my excellent beauty.",
 "translation": "The jasmine buds have matured. Along with the jasmine, the wide forest fields of the cool rainy season have reached the fullness of their beauty. Yet, he who caused my bright ornaments to slip loose has not returned. And now, the evening has arrived, aiming to destroy my precious beauty.",
 "modern": "முல்லை மொட்டுகள் மலரத் தொடங்கிவிட்டன. முல்லை மலர்களோடு குளிர்ந்த கார்காலத்தின் அகன்ற முல்லை நிலமும் தன் அழகில் முழுமை பெற்றுவிட்டது. என் ஒளிபொருந்திய அணிகலன்கள் நெகிழ்ந்து கழலும்படி செய்தவர் இன்னும் வரவில்லை. ஆனால், என் சிறந்த அழகை அழிப்பதை நோக்கமாகக் கொண்டு மாலைப் பொழுது வந்துவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her companion during the rainy season, which is the time her lover promised to return. She observes that the jasmine buds are blooming and the entire forest landscape is lush and beautiful with the arrival of the rains. However, her lover, whose absence causes her to waste away and makes her ornaments slip off, has not yet returned. Instead, the painful evening hour has arrived, which seems to specifically target and threaten her remaining vitality. This poem belongs to the mullai tiṇai, which is characterized by the theme of patient waiting during the rainy season.",
